+## 11. Visual acceptance: strict, evidence based
+
+Create `output/uiphase2-implementation/visual-matrix.md` with 29 rows before coding. Fields: reference ID/hash/dimensions, actual route/state/fixture IDs, reference region, viewport/device scale, screenshot path, measured landmarks, exact differences, disposition, reviewer. Settings composite rows link multiple actual captures; split top/lower references can share one route with separate scroll positions. No coverage by inference.
+
+For each image:
+
+1. Open the reference and actual browser screenshot side by side at matching effective width. Use real browser captures; never generate a replacement “after” image.
+2. Match page frame, header height, title position, main/rail widths, card order, section spacing, primary control placement, type hierarchy, borders, radii and semantic surfaces. Inspect upper and lower controls. No skipped lower forms.
+3. Measure at least five landmarks: content left/right edges, headline baseline/top, first section top, rail/column boundary, primary action position. Initial tolerance after scale calibration: major edges within 8 CSS px; column proportions within 3 percentage points; heading/body font sizes within 2 CSS px of the frozen visual target. These are review tolerances, not measurements already performed. Large content-induced height shifts must be explained, not hidden by arbitrary cropping.
+4. Use matched-length synthetic content where useful for geometry, but never transplant unsupported legal copy. Compare structure separately from real text wrapping. Record font rendering differences; do not use those as an excuse for wrong layout.
+5. Pixel-difference overlays can help diagnose spacing but cannot alone pass/fail this generated-raster reference. Text rendering and illustrative content differ. Sol must inspect the actual images and use the landmarks plus control inventory.
+6. A missing section, wrong column structure, legacy dense layout, wrong headline font, broad heavy fills, changed action hierarchy, or hidden control is a material failure. Fix it before completion even if tests pass.
+
+No new approval stage is needed for routine visual corrections. Sol checks against the selected design, not personal taste. Any accepted deviation is logged against one of section 7's allowed reasons or a documented material conflict.
+
+Responsive checks: one wide desktop at 1440 CSS px, the matched native-width references, a 768px layout, and a 390px narrow layout for representative families: Today, board/table, reader/rail, long editor, Settings. Exercise every shared navigation mode. Verify no unintended page-wide horizontal overflow; an explicitly labelled table scroll region is allowed. Check long source titles/URLs, empty sections and error text. The user waived 200% zoom: skip it and record “waived by user”, not passed. Do not depend on an unlocked Mac; prefer the Codex in-app browser. Discover supported viewport/capture APIs rather than inventing them. If a real resize is unavailable, use another available browser automation surface and report it; CSS scaling a screenshot does not prove responsive behavior.
+
+Keyboard checks: Tab/Shift+Tab in logical order; visible focus; Enter/Space activates buttons/disclosures/tabs; Escape closes a preview/menu where applicable; focus returns to the triggering citation/control; native labels associate with inputs; keyboard stage movement; no focus trap or hidden focused element. Honor reduced-motion preferences for any introduced transition; check that no new animation is required to understand state. Check representative components once and each distinct interaction, not 29 identical repetitions.
